{"activity_text": "Anti-Gram+ & Gram-, Antifungal, candidacidal, Chemotactic", "article_id": "", "assay_text": "Unknown", "assay_type": "", "comments_text": "A chemokine that initiates chemotaxis via the ChemR23 G protein-coupled seven transmemrbane receptor. Truncation of the C-terminal six residues is required to activate peptide chemotactic activity. While chemS157 (with C-terminal residue 158-163 removed) and chemR125 (with C-terminal 126-163 removed) are equally antibacterial against E. coli and K. pneumoniae, chemR125 is not chemotactic. In a subsequent study using overlapping synthetic peptides, the major antibacterial region of chemerin was mapped to the segment Val(66)-Pro(85), which causes direct bacterial lysis (see PLoS One. 2013;8(3):e58709). The MIC values of this p4 fragment is 12.5 uM against S. aureus, 3.1-6.3 uM against E. coli, 6.3 uM P. aeruginosa, 6.3 uM against C. albicans.", "concentration": "", "cytotoxicity_text": "", "hemolytic_activity_text": "", "measure_group": "", "measure_value": "", "pubmed_id": "23527010", "record_granularity": "entry_text", "reference_text": "J Immunol. 2011 Aug 1;187(3):1403-10. Pub-Med.", "sequence_key": "APD6:AP02195", "source_id": "AP02195", "source_numeric_id": "02195", "source_path": "APD6/apd6_export/structured/peptides.csv", "source_record_id": "AP02195", "source_table": "peptides.csv", "stability_text": "", "subject_name": "", "target_organism_text": "", "title": "Regulation of chemerin chemoattractant and antibacterial activity by human cysteine cathepsins.", "unit": "", "﻿database": "APD6"}
